---
source: tests/snapshots_help_contracts.rs
expression: "help(&scratch, None, &[\"--help\", \"issue\", \"close\"])"
---
Usage: issue close <NUMBER> [--worktree-session SESSION]

Close the issue on the provider. Redmine preflight: when the issue has open native children (subtasks), the close fails before any status write with a bounded list of child identity/title/status; close each child individually first, then retry the parent. Phasegent never cascade-closes children. Only after the remote close succeeds are the active worktree leases matching the resolved repo identity and this issue flipped to `retained` across every session; the release needs no session identity. --worktree-session, else PHASEGENT_SESSION_ID, is optional and only names the closer in the release_reason ("issue closed: <session>"); without either, the reason is the plain "issue closed" and no owner is guessed. A failed remote close leaves every local lease untouched, and other issues and repo identities are never affected. After the flip the close removes this issue's worktree directories in the resolved repo when the directory is clean (uncommitted or untracked files count as dirty, except a verified root `.cache`-only worktree: a real root `.cache` directory with no tracked paths below it, porcelain status naming only `??` paths under that exact root, and a bounded symlink-free inventory of reliable device/inode and high-resolution change evidence re-read identically immediately before a guarded cache removal, with the initial eligibility baseline carried across session movement and a platform without reliable identity failing closed; any other untracked, tracked, staged, modified, renamed, unmerged, nested, symlinked, cross-filesystem, unreadable, over-bound, same-length or root-replaced, or otherwise changed content keeps the directory; every applicable local, lease, closure, and fresh host gate is re-checked after cache clearance before the ordinary no-force worktree removal runs), no active lease of another session points at it, and it is not the repository's main checkout; a kept directory emits one stderr warning naming the guard that kept it.

A directory whose lease belongs to an OpenCode session (`ses_…`) is protected until the host confirms the session is no longer hosted there. The close reads that directory's occupancy and the lineage the host reports for it, and returns the idle members of its lease family — the sessions the leases name and the descendants that descend from them — to the repository's verified main checkout with `opencode api session.move`: every safe family member is requested once before any confirmation wait, pending requests are then confirmed with fresh reads, and a close keeps the directory without waiting when any request remains pending. A session of another family, and a running session other than the closing session the close is finishing, keeps the directory and is never moved. Anything unproven keeps the directory and warns: an unreachable API, an incomplete listing, a session the instance does not own, an unreadable, mismatched, or cyclic lineage, or a move that has not taken effect yet. A pure CLI worktree never reaches the API. The other issues the directory is associated with are each read through their own durable task and must be confirmed closed; a legacy number without a durable task keeps the configured-provider resolution, and an open, ambiguous, or unreadable one keeps the directory. The close never waits for the move; `issue sync` removes a deferred directory once the host reports it free. Branches and lease rows are never deleted, and the cleanup is best-effort: it never changes the exit code or the stdout document.

After that cleanup succeeds, the close may return the repository's verified main checkout to the branch it was on before the automatic issue-branch switch and fast-forward only that branch's own configured upstream. It acts only when a matching baseline exists, the main checkout is clean (untracked files count as dirty), it is on the captured issue branch or the already-restored baseline, no active repository lease or other hosted OpenCode session occupies it, and the closing session identity is verifiable. Restoration uses no force, stash, reset, clean, branch deletion, or merge commit. A missing baseline is a quiet no-op. A deleted captured branch (never recreated from a remote-tracking namesake), dirty or unknown state, third-party branch, unresolvable main checkout with recorded baseline evidence, local ahead/diverged state, unavailable remote, or refused fast-forward leaves the checkout and commits untouched and warns. A skipped or failed upstream update is retried by repeating `issue close`; once restored and updated, or when no configured upstream exists, a repeat close is a no-op without another fetch. This best-effort restoration never changes the close's stdout or exit code.

Close an issue
